Osza Browser - Changelog
========================

Unreleased
---------
The visual refresh: one icon language everywhere. Security tools: the
start of the bundled security suite.

Fixed:
- The browser crashed on every launch and no window ever appeared:
  MainApplication called OszaIcons::activateTheme() while still inside
  its constructor, before Settings::createSettings() had run, and
  activateTheme() dereferenced the not-yet-created global settings
  object (a guaranteed nullptr crash on every platform, fresh or
  existing profile). The call now happens after the settings exist and
  activateTheme() is null-safe on top, falling back to palette
  detection when called before them.

Added:
- Security tools (1): HTTPS-Only mode. When enabled (Preferences >
  Privacy > Security tools), every insecure http:// request is
  transparently upgraded to https:// by a new request interceptor -
  top-level navigations as well as subresources, before anything
  touches the network. Local addresses (localhost, loopback), private
  network ranges (10/8, 172.16/12, 192.168/16, 169.254/16, IPv6 ULA
  and link-local, .local and .localhost names) are never upgraded, and
  a per-site exception list ("HTTPS-Only exceptions" dialog) covers
  the servers that only exist over plain HTTP.
- Security tools (2): tracking-parameter stripping, on by default.
  Known click-identifier query parameters (utm_*, fbclid, gclid,
  msclkid, twclid, ttclid, _hsenc, s_kwcid and friends - 33 names) are
  removed from every http(s) request before it is sent, so links that
  were shared or pasted with campaign trackers load clean. Signed CDN
  URLs are untouched (they do not use these parameters) and the
  feature can be turned off next to HTTPS-Only mode.
Changed:
- Every icon in the product was redrawn as one original, coherent set
  (see ICONS.md for the system). The browser now ships a complete
  bundled "Osza" icon theme - a light and a dark variant covering all
  81 themed icon names the code requests, plus hand-tuned 16 px versions
  of the high-traffic toolbar glyphs - so no toolbar, menu, sidebar,
  preferences page or dialog shows blank or mismatched icons anymore,
  on any platform and in light or dark mode.
- Osza's UI icons no longer follow the desktop icon theme on Linux:
  the bundled theme is active on all platforms and the system theme is
  only a fallback for names Osza does not ship. The browser therefore
  looks identical on Linux, Windows and macOS. This is a deliberate
  behavior change; a restart is required after switching palettes,
  like the chrome themes.
- The tab close button is no longer a saturated red cross: a quiet
  neutral cross, a darker hover state and a red pressed confirmation,
  each legible on light and dark chrome. Toolbar hover overlays no
  longer use hard-coded black translucency (which was invisible on
  dark palettes) and follow the active palette instead.
- The internal pages' artwork (start page, about, restore, crash and
  the speed dial buttons), the preferences page icons, the site info
  dialog, the loading spinner and every plugin glyph were redrawn on
  the same system. The start page and about art now use the Osza ring
  and wordmark instead of leftover artwork from the project's origins.
- App and packaging art (hicolor set, Windows .ico, macOS .icns, DMG
  background, document-type icons, README banner) was regenerated from
  the brand mark, with a simplified variant for 32 px and below.

Removed:
- The "breeze-fallback" icon theme (17 hard-coded dark-grey icons),
  the unused legacy location bar PNGs, the unused third-party speed
  dial backgrounds and three orphaned mac theme images.

Added:
- tools/icons: a design system, a deterministic build pipeline and a
  static auditor for icons and resources (development tools only, no
  new build or runtime dependencies). ICONS.md documents the grid,
  palette, states and how to add an icon.

Fixed:
- The bookmark editor's speed dial button showed no icon at all: it
  referenced a resource that never existed (:/icons/theme/
  speeddial.png) and now uses a proper glyph.
- The Linux build check failed to compile: oszaicons.cpp called
  Settings::globalSettings()->value() without including <QSettings>,
  leaving the type incomplete (the -Werror=return-type error on top
  was a cascade of that).
- The README banner and the DMG background lost the bottom of the
  wordmark: the icon pipeline's svg_to_png() defaulted the output
  height to the width, which letterboxed the 494x132 brand lockup
  into a square canvas and pushed the wordmark below the banners'
  bottom edges. The README logo is now shown at 360 px so the mark
  and wordmark are legible.
- CI: the snap release job retries once on a clean managed instance
  before failing, because snapcraft's LXD build VM intermittently
  fails its initial apt update ("Failed to refresh package list").

1.2  (2026-11-01)
-----------------
The modern chrome refresh release.

Added:
- New bundled "Osza Cyber" theme (Preferences -> Themes) applying the
  design system of the internal pages to the browser chrome: rounded
  tab cards with a neon cyan top edge, dark pill-shaped address and
  search fields with cyan focus rings, dark menus with cyan-tinted
  hover states, a bundled dark icon set referenced directly so the
  dark chrome never gets light artwork, and a private-browsing
  variant that shifts the accent to violet (private.css).

Changed:
- The default Windows, macOS and Linux themes were modernized into a
  shared "modern neutral" chrome: one tinted canvas behind the tab
  strip, rounded selected-tab cards that connect to the toolbar band,
  flat toolbar buttons with quiet hover states, pill-shaped location
  and search fields with a teal focus ring matching the in-app accent,
  rounded menus, a separated status bar and larger hit areas for the
  tab close button and the strip controls. Every color resolves from
  the active palette, so the chrome follows the system light and dark
  scheme automatically. The macOS theme now resolves icons through
  the theme icon mechanism (with the bundled fallbacks) instead of
  the legacy four-state PNG artwork.

Fixed:
- CI: the KDE Neon apt repository is now fetched from the CDN-backed
  archive.neon.kde.org endpoint over HTTPS instead of the single
  origin.archive.neon.kde.org backend server, which intermittently
  refuses connections and broke the Linux build check on 2026-10-31
  (the two endpoints serve a byte-identical archive). The snap recipe
  points at the same endpoint, the workflows fall back to the origin
  server only if the CDN cannot be reached, and the dependency
  installer now passes apt retry options so transient network blips
  no longer abort a run.

0.1.1  (2026-10-27)
-------------------
First release published under the Osza Browser identity.

Changed:
- Complete rename of every part of the project from Veex to Osza:
  namespace, classes, identifiers, files, build targets, icons,
  translation catalogs and application identity.
- New application identity: org.osza.OszaBrowser, executable "osza".
- QML module URI is now org.osza; Python plugin symbols are now
  pyosza_load_plugin / pyosza_init_plugin.
- Desktop, appstream, snap, flatpak, AppImage and CI packaging updated
  to the new names, paths and osza-tools GitHub links.
- New brand identity "Orbit": the lowercase o of osza drawn as a bold
  open ring with an orbiting comet dot on a deep-space squircle, in a
  mint-cyan-indigo gradient anchored on the in-app accent (#00ffd1).
  Includes the full "osza" wordmark (Quicksand Bold letterforms) used
  by the start page and the about dialog with proper light/dark logo
  variants, flat black/white emblem variants, complete icon sets
  (hicolor, Windows .ico, macOS .icns) and the DMG installer icon,
  replacing the temporary gold medallion.

Fixed:
- Release CI: Windows packages are now built against Qt 6.8 LTS with
  the MSVC 2022 archives instead of Qt 6.7 (MSVC 2019), pulling in the
  much newer Chromium that the current 6.8 patch releases bundle.
- Release CI: macOS packaging no longer aborts in the bundle step by
  writing a multi-line value (find output) to GITHUB_ENV, which GitHub
  Actions rejects; the unused variable was removed.
- Build: define REQUIRED_QT_VERSION so the Qt >= 6.9 Qt6NetworkPrivate
  lookup gets a proper version constraint instead of an empty variable.
- Release CI: fetch extra-cmake-modules and KF6 Archive from the KDE
  GitHub mirrors on Windows; the download.kde.org CDN stalls caused
  hour-long curl retry loops on the release runners.
- CI: new "Build check" workflow that compiles the tree (including
  autotests and benchmark targets) on every push to main and on pull
  requests, so build breakage no longer waits for a release tag.

0.1.0  (2026-10-23)
-------------------
First public release of Osza Browser.

Changed:
- Rebranded from Falkon (KDE) with a deep rename of the codebase:
  namespace, classes, identifiers, files, build targets and catalogs.
- New application identity: org.osza.OszaBrowser, executable "osza".
- New temporary logo and icon set (gradient V on dark diamond).
- Replaced upstream KDE service links with the Osza Tools GitHub project.
- Default bookmarks and speed dial now point to Osza Tools resources.
- Renamed the KDE Frameworks Integration plugin to System Integration.
- Removed KDE CI configuration files.

Fixed:
- Speed Dial thumbnails are now written atomically (encode to memory,
  commit via QSaveFile) so an interrupted save can no longer leave a
  truncated PNG on disk.
- Page thumbnailer loads its QML with the canonical qrc:/ resource URL.

Packaging:
- Cross-platform release CI: Linux tar.gz, Linux snap, macOS dmg and
  Windows zip are built and attached to every v* tag release.
- Added BUILD_TRANSLATIONS build option so platforms without Ki18n
  (e.g. Windows) can skip the po-installation requirement.
- Repository-local dependency installer: linux/install-build-deps.sh.

Notes:
- Osza Browser is based on Falkon, licensed under GPL-3.0-or-later.
- Original Falkon authors: David Rosca, Juraj Oravec and all
  Falkon/KDE contributors - thank you.
